KG42.register("rules-bed49190bd33ed545f1d85f7",{"id":"37-dsm_auction_incentives::v141-c-R006","paper":"37-dsm_auction_incentives","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R006","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Market payment","process_stage":"settlement","market_meaning":"The declared demand-parameter vector, including requested consumption vectors and declared consumption bounds, is an input to the payment function. Scope: Method 2 declared-demand input to payment rule (20) used in the enhanced AGV mechanism. Registered users submit next-cycle energy demand in step 1 before the provider sets payments in step 2. Scope: The recurring enhanced AGV pricing procedure in Table I. The payment rule evaluates its utility-and-cost component using consumption allocations supplied by the social-welfare allocation rule. Under fixed daily consumption, that allocation calculation reduces to cost minimization. Scope: Allocation-dependent payment rule (20) in the AGV construction used by enhanced AGV. Condition: In the implemented reduction from (18) to (19), each user's declared daily consumption is held unchanged and only its hourly distribution is optimized. A user with a recorded history of cheating must pay an additional penalty in following operation cycles. Scope: History-based punishment in the enhanced AGV mechanism. Condition: The user's consumption history contains a cheating record. Users obtaining higher utility from the same unit of energy have to pay more. Scope: The source's utility-based AGV payment discussion. Condition: Comparing utility gained from the same unit of energy. The transfer-payment component reallocates payments among users without requiring extra payment from the system. Scope: The enhanced AGV transfer-payment component, rather than the total electricity bills.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function settlement.payment under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["bidding.demand","clearing.social_welfare","settlement.payment"],"E":[{"edge_id":"v141-c-R006-E001","source":"bidding.demand","target":"settlement.payment","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R006-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e005","v141-b-g02-e008"]},{"edge_id":"v141-c-R006-E002","source":"bidding.demand","target":"settlement.payment","type":"precedes","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R006-C002"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e011"]},{"edge_id":"v141-c-R006-E003","source":"clearing.social_welfare","target":"settlement.payment","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R006-C003"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e001","v141-b-g02-e006","v141-b-g02-e007","v141-b-g02-e008"]}],"omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"Allocation-dependent payment rule (20) in the AGV construction used by enhanced AGV. | History-based punishment in the enhanced AGV mechanism. | Method 2 declared-demand input to payment rule (20) used in the enhanced AGV mechanism. | The enhanced AGV transfer-payment component, rather than the total electricity bills. | The recurring enhanced AGV pricing procedure in Table I. | The source's utility-based AGV payment discussion.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e03","v141-b-g02-e001","v141-b-g02-e005","v141-b-g02-e006","v141-b-g02-e007","v141-b-g02-e008","v141-b-g02-e011","v141-b-g02-e017","v141-b-g02-e021"]},"x":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R006-VAR-x_i_s","symbol":"x_i^s","meaning":"Power consumption of user i in time slot s, as selected by the social-welfare consumption allocation and evaluated in the payment rule.","kind":"operational","domain":"x_i^s ∈ [b_i^s, B_i^s] for all s ∈ S; equivalently x_i ∈ X_i","unit":null,"indices":["i","s"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e08","v141-b-g02-e006","v141-b-g02-e008"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R006-VAR-t_i","symbol":"t_i","meaning":"Payment amount of user i under payment rule (20).","kind":"operational","domain":"unspecified in source (payment amount)","unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e008","v141-b-g02-e011"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R006-VAR-ts_i","symbol":"ts_i","meaning":"Transfer-payment component of user i in payment rule (20).","kind":"operational","domain":"\\mathbb{R}","unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e008","v141-b-g02-e017","v141-b-g01-e11"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R006-VAR-ts_i_enhanced","symbol":"ts_i^{enhanced}","meaning":"Enhanced AGV transfer payment of user i, including the cheat-cost component.","kind":"operational","domain":"\\mathbb{R}","unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e013","v141-b-g02-e014"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R006-VAR-ts_i_norm","symbol":"ts_i^{norm}","meaning":"Normal AGV transfer payment of user i, used as the base component of the enhanced transfer payment.","kind":"operational","domain":"\\mathbb{R}","unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e010","v141-b-g02-e013"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R006-VAR-x_i_total","symbol":"x_i","meaning":"User i's real total daily power consumption, as recorded after consumption and used in the cheat-penalty term.","kind":"operational","domain":"daily energy consumption; range unspecified in source","unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e014","v141-b-g02-e015"]}],"theta":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R006-PAR-theta_hat","symbol":"\\hat{\\theta}","meaning":"Profile of declared demand parameters submitted by users; under Method 2 each user's component is \\hat{\\theta}_i=\\{\\hat{\\mathbf B}_i,\\hat{\\mathbf b}_i,\\hat{\\mathbf x}_i\\}, with declared consumption bounds and vector.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"\\hat{\\theta}=\\{\\hat{\\theta}_1,\\ldots,\\hat{\\theta}_N\\}"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e005","v141-b-g02-e008","v141-b-g02-e011"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R006-PAR-theta_minus_i","symbol":"\\theta_{-i}","meaning":"Profile of information of all users other than i, as used in the budget-balance and transfer-payment expressions.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"\\theta_{-i}"},"unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e014","v141-b-g02-e017"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R006-PAR-U_m","symbol":"U_m","meaning":"Utility function of user m, mapping total consumption to satisfaction or benefit.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"U_m(\\cdot)"},"unit":null,"indices":["m"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e001","v141-b-g02-e008"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R006-PAR-C_s","symbol":"C_s","meaning":"Cost function for providing aggregate energy in time slot s.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"C_s(\\cdot)"},"unit":null,"indices":["s"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g02-e001","v141-b-g02-e008"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R006-PAR-h_i","symbol":"h_i","meaning":"Arbitrary function of other users' declared parameters in payment rule (20); defined by equation (21) in the source.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"h_i(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i})"},"unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e008","v141-b-g02-e009"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R006-PAR-N","symbol":"N","meaning":"Number of users, N=|\\mathcal{N}|.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"N=|\\mathcal{N}|"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e07","v141-b-g02-e017"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R006-PAR-x_hat_i","symbol":"\\hat{x}_i","meaning":"User i's declared total amount of daily power consumption, used in the cheat-penalty term.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"\\hat{x}_i"},"unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e014","v141-b-g02-e015"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R006-PAR-r_1","symbol":"r_1","meaning":"Parameter controlling the influence of the normal AGV transfer payment in the enhanced transfer payment.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e013","v141-b-g02-e021"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R006-PAR-ct_i","symbol":"ct_i","meaning":"Cheat-cost function for user i, depending on other users' true and declared information; its explicit equation (24) is not formalized because the supplied transcription has ambiguous indices.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"ct_i(\\theta_{-i},\\hat{\\theta}_{-i})"},"unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e013","v141-b-g02-e014"]}],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The declared demand-parameter vector, including requested consumption vectors and declared consumption bounds, is an input to the payment function. Scope: Method 2 declared-demand input to payment rule (20) used in the enhanced AGV mechanism.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.demand","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e005","v141-b-g02-e008"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Registered users submit next-cycle energy demand in step 1 before the provider sets payments in step 2. Scope: The recurring enhanced AGV pricing procedure in Table I.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.demand","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e011"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The payment rule evaluates its utility-and-cost component using consumption allocations supplied by the social-welfare allocation rule. Under fixed daily consumption, that allocation calculation reduces to cost minimization. Scope: Allocation-dependent payment rule (20) in the AGV construction used by enhanced AGV. Condition: In the implemented reduction from (18) to (19), each user's declared daily consumption is held unchanged and only its hourly distribution is optimized.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.social_welfare","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e001","v141-b-g02-e006","v141-b-g02-e007","v141-b-g02-e008"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C004","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"A user with a recorded history of cheating must pay an additional penalty in following operation cycles. Scope: History-based punishment in the enhanced AGV mechanism. Condition: The user's consumption history contains a cheating record.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e03"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C005","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Users obtaining higher utility from the same unit of energy have to pay more. Scope: The source's utility-based AGV payment discussion. Condition: Comparing utility gained from the same unit of energy.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e021"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C006","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The transfer-payment component reallocates payments among users without requiring extra payment from the system. Scope: The enhanced AGV transfer-payment component, rather than the total electricity bills.","expression":"\\sum_{i=1}^{N} ts_i(\\theta_{-i}) = 0","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R006-VAR-ts_i"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R006-PAR-N","v141-c-R006-PAR-theta_minus_i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e017"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C007","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Payment rule (20): the payment to user i equals the negative of other users' aggregate utility minus total cost at the allocation induced by declared demand, plus an arbitrary function h_i of other users' declared parameters and a transfer payment ts_i of other users' declared parameters. Scope: Payment rule (20) in the enhanced AGV mechanism.","expression":"t_i(\\hat{\\theta}) = -\\left[ \\sum_{m\\in\\mathcal{N}_{-i}} U_m\\left(\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}} x_m^s(\\hat{\\theta})\\right) - \\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}} C_s\\left(\\sum_{m\\in\\mathcal{N}} x_m^s(\\hat{\\theta})\\right) \\right] + h_i(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}) + ts_i(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i})","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.demand","clearing.social_welfare","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R006-VAR-x_i_s","v141-c-R006-VAR-t_i","v141-c-R006-VAR-ts_i"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R006-PAR-theta_hat","v141-c-R006-PAR-U_m","v141-c-R006-PAR-C_s","v141-c-R006-PAR-h_i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e008","v141-b-g02-e011"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C008","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The cheat-penalty term φ(-i) equals the square of the positive aggregate over-declaration shortfall of users other than i, and equals zero otherwise. Scope: Equation (25), used in the enhanced AGV transfer payment (23)-(24).","expression":"\\phi(-i) = \\begin{cases} \\left[\\sum_{m\\in\\mathcal{N}_{-i}}(x_m-\\hat{x}_m)\\right]^2 & \\text{if } \\left[\\sum_{m\\in\\mathcal{N}_{-i}}(x_m-\\hat{x}_m)\\right]>0, \\\\ 0 & \\text{else}. \\end{cases}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.demand","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R006-VAR-x_i_total"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R006-PAR-x_hat_i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e015","v141-b-g02-e014"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C009","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The enhanced transfer payment is the weighted normal AGV transfer payment plus a cheat-cost function: ts_i^{enhanced}(\\hat\\theta_{-i}) = r_1 ts_i^{norm}(\\hat\\theta_{-i}) + ct_i(\\theta_{-i},\\hat\\theta_{-i}), where r_1 controls the basic transfer payment and ct_i sets the cost to cheat. Scope: Enhanced transfer payment (23); the explicit form of ct_i in (24) is left unresolved.","expression":"ts_i^{enhanced}(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}) = r_1 \\cdot ts_i^{norm}(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}) + ct_i(\\theta_{-i}, \\hat{\\theta}_{-i})","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.demand","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R006-VAR-ts_i_enhanced","v141-c-R006-VAR-ts_i_norm"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R006-PAR-r_1","v141-c-R006-PAR-ct_i","v141-c-R006-PAR-theta_minus_i","v141-c-R006-PAR-theta_hat"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e013","v141-b-g02-e014"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e01","location":{"location_id":"src-d83df5b2d1-p01-c1r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"Abstract","anchor_text":"p g g allow more information exchange between users and providers, en and they can meet users’ energ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Based on this, we propose an enhanced AGV (Arrow-d'Aspremont-Gerard-Varet) mechanism to ensure truthfulness. In this incentive method, user's payment is related to its consumption credit. One will be punished to pay extra if there is a cheat record in its consumption history. We prove that the enhanced AGV mechanism can achieve the basic qualifications: incentive compatibility, individual rationality and budget balance."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e03","location":{"location_id":"src-d83df5b2d1-p02-c1r3__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. Introduction","anchor_text":"user cannot beneﬁt from reselling planned power). So there is Fi mainly one kind of cheat, i.e., the","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"So there is mainly one kind of cheat, i.e., the user claims lower demand and consumes more. To detect the cheat, we compare user's real consumption with its claimed one. We assume the smart meter can record user's claimed and real consumption information and keep it for a certain period. With the AGV mechanism, the power consumption game can reach a Bayesian Nash equilibrium [16] when all users reveal true demand. User who reveals false information will have to pay much more in the following operation cycles. Considering the only kind of cheat, we revise the transfer payment in the AGV mechanism to adapt our system."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e001","location":{"location_id":"src-d83df5b2d1-p05-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Problem Formulation and Mechanism Solution / B. Problem Formulation","anchor_text":"MA et al.: INCENTIVE MECHANISM FOR DEMAND SIDE MANAGEMENT IN SMART GR B. Problem Formulation co tru ","equation_id":"(15)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Since we apply the utility function to represent users’ benefits through electricity consumption, we can use the sum of all users’ utility functions to indicate the social benefit of electricity consumption. Hence, we consider a social objective, which is to maximize the social benefit while minimizing the cost of providing that amount of electricity. This can be represented by the following problem: \\[ \\max_{\\mathbf{x}_i \\in \\mathcal{X}_i, i\\in\\mathcal{N}} \\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{N}} U_i\\left(\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}} x_i^s\\right) - \\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}} C_s\\left(\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{N}} x_i^s\\right), \\tag{15} \\] where \\mathbf{x}_i is user i’s power consumption vector, U_i(\\cdot) is as in (14), and C_s(\\cdot) is as in (2). The proposed energy consumption schedule is the solution of (15). Meanwhile, problem (15) is a concave maximization, which can be solved by convex programming such as the interior point method (IPM) [29]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e005","location":{"location_id":"src-d83df5b2d1-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Problem Formulation and Mechanism Solution / C. Proposed Mechanism","anchor_text":"gy y p g y 5) declaring false utility parameters (to be shown in the following simulation result usi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Method 2: The utility parameter, rather than declared by each user, is calculated based on each user’s consumptive plan. We classify users into different types, e.g., industrial user, office building user, shopping market user, and residential user. Different types of users will gain different utilities while consuming energy in the same way. In order to prevent users’ cheating by declaring false utility parameters (to be shown in the following simulation result using Method 1), we classify the electrical appliances into certain types based on their average utilities for users of the same type. When declaring the consumptive information, besides \\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_i, the users have to declare what types of appliances they are going to use and the planned consumption demand of each type. \\psi_i is calculated based on the user type, appliance type and the planned consumption demand. We define \\theta_i = \\{\\mathbf{B}_i, \\mathbf{b}_i, \\mathbf{x}_i\\} and \\hat{\\theta}_i = \\{\\hat{\\mathbf{B}}_i, \\hat{\\mathbf{b}}_i, \\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_i\\}, respectively, to denote the true and declared demand parameters, where \\hat{\\mathbf{B}}_i, \\hat{\\mathbf{b}}_i, and \\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_i are the declared values for \\mathbf{B}_i, \\mathbf{b}_i, and \\mathbf{x}_i, respectively."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e006","location":{"location_id":"src-d83df5b2d1-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Problem Formulation and Mechanism Solution / C. Proposed Mechanism","anchor_text":". 2) Social Objective: We deﬁne the social objective in the as form of 6) where is the set of all us","equation_id":"(18)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"3) Optimal Consumption Vector: The optimal consumptive vector is set by the allocation rule \\mathbf{x}(\\hat{\\theta}) defined as follows. For a given vector of the declared demand information \\hat{\\theta} = \\{\\hat{\\theta}_1,\\ldots,\\hat{\\theta}_N\\}, the AGV mechanism chooses the consumption allocation \\mathbf{x}(\\hat{\\theta}) as the optimal solution to (15), and it calculates the optimal consumption vectors as \\[ \\mathbf{x}(\\hat{\\theta}) = \\arg\\max_{\\mathbf{x}_i \\in \\mathcal{X}_i, i\\in\\mathcal{N}} \\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{N}} U_i\\left(\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}} x_i^s\\right) - \\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}} C_s\\left(\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{N}} x_i^s\\right). \\tag{18} \\]"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e007","location":{"location_id":"src-d83df5b2d1-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Problem Formulation and Mechanism Solution / C. Proposed Mechanism","anchor_text":"(18) 7) We suppose that the energy provider does not change the de- clared amount of every user’s da","equation_id":"(19), (20)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"We suppose that the energy provider does not change the declared amount of every user’s daily power consumption. We only optimize users’ hourly consumptive distribution. Thus, the problem in (18) can be reduced to \\[ \\mathbf{x}(\\hat{\\theta}) = \\arg\\min_{\\mathbf{x}_i \\in \\mathcal{X}_i, i\\in\\mathcal{N}} \\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}} C_s\\left(\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{N}} x_i^s\\right). \\tag{19} \\]"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e008","location":{"location_id":"src-d83df5b2d1-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Problem Formulation and Mechanism Solution / C. Proposed Mechanism","anchor_text":"(18) 7) We suppose that the energy provider does not change the de- clared amount of every user’s da","equation_id":"(19), (20)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"4) Payment: The payments are structured such that \\[ t_i(\\hat{\\theta}) = -\\left[ \\sum_{m\\in\\mathcal{N}_{-i}} U_m\\left(\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}} x_m^s(\\hat{\\theta})\\right) - \\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}} C_s\\left(\\sum_{m\\in\\mathcal{N}} x_m^s(\\hat{\\theta})\\right) \\right] + h_i(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}) + ts_i(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}), \\tag{20} \\] where h_i is an arbitrary function of \\hat{\\theta}_{-i} and ts_i(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}) is the transfer payment of user i. We use the following function (which is introduced in [31]) to be h_i."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e011","location":{"location_id":"src-d83df5b2d1-p06-c2r1__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Problem Formulation and Mechanism Solution / C. Proposed Mechanism","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON SMART GRID, VOL. 5, NO. 3, MAY 2014 he TABLE I ch PRICING METHOD USING THE ENHA","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":"Table I","image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"TABLE I PRICING METHOD USING THE ENHANCED AGV MECHANISM 1. Registered users submit the energy demand for the next consumption cycle. * Each user selects the types of appliances and decides each type’s energy demand. The smart meter computes \\hat{x}_i and \\hat{\\psi}_i. * \\hat{\\theta}_i = \\{\\mathbf{B}_i, \\mathbf{b}_i, \\mathbf{x}_i\\}, \\psi_i and user’s consumption record will be submitted to the energy provider. 2. The energy provider optimizes users’ consumption allocations and set the payments. * Optimize users’ consumption allocations based on the submitted information by solving (19). * Compute the values of users’ payments using (20) and (23). * Announce the recommended consumption allocations and payments to users. 3. The users start the new consumption cycle, and the smart meters record users’ consumption information. Return to 1."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e017","location":{"location_id":"src-d83df5b2d1-p07-c1r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Problem Formulation and Mechanism Solution / C. Proposed Mechanism","anchor_text":"no (26) [3 tai If we calculate the total transfer payment of all users, we can get th th pa slo A. b","equation_id":"(26)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"If we calculate the total transfer payment of all users, we can get \\[ \\sum_{i=1}^{N} ts_i(\\theta_{-i}) = r_1\\left[ \\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\Phi(-i) - \\frac{1}{N-1}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\sum_{j=1,j\\neq i}^{N}\\Phi(-j) \\right] + r_2\\left[ \\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\phi(-i) - \\frac{1}{N-1}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\sum_{j=1,j\\neq i}^{N}\\phi(-j) \\right] = r_1\\left[ \\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\Phi(-i) - \\sum_{j=1}^{N}\\Phi(-j) \\right] + r_2\\left[ \\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\phi(-i) - \\sum_{j=1}^{N}\\phi(-j) \\right] = 0. \\] This implies that the transfer function can realize a payment reallocation among users, and no extra payment is required to be paid by the system."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e021","location":{"location_id":"src-d83df5b2d1-p08-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"V. Simulation Results / B. Relationships in AGV Mechanism","anchor_text":"mechanism can achieve budget balance. Hence, the AGV mech- 17 anism can also effectively achieve the","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 4, Fig. 5","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In the AGV mechanism, we add a transfer payment as a function of the effect of user’s utility value, to user’s payment. As illustrated in Fig. 4, it is the utility value of the user, rather than the power consumption that decides the payment of the user. In Fig. 5, we can find out the close relation between the transfer payments and users’ parameters of the utility value. Compared to the effect by the marginal cost, the transfer payment plays the main role in controlling user’s payment. This means, in the AGV mechanism, those who gain higher utilities by using the same unit of energy have to pay more. We can use a parameter r_1 to control the effect of the utility value."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e07","location":{"location_id":"src-d83df5b2d1-p03-c1r1__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model / A. DSM Scenario in the SG","anchor_text":"MA et al.: INCENTIVE MECHANISM FOR DEMAND SIDE MANAGEMENT IN SMART GR the next-operation-cycle load ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"1) User: Let \\mathcal{N} = \\{1,2,\\ldots,N\\} denote the set of all users, and N \\triangleq |\\mathcal{N}|. 2) Time Slot: The grid system operates cyclically and each operation cycle is divided into S time slots. Let \\mathcal{S} = \\{1,2,\\ldots,S\\} denote the set of all time slots in one operation cycle. We have S \\triangleq |\\mathcal{S}|. According to user's different power demand in different time, we have the on-peak, off-peak, and mid-peak time slots in a time division."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e08","location":{"location_id":"src-d83df5b2d1-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model / A. DSM Scenario in the SG","anchor_text":"consumption of user in time slot . We deﬁne as the vector of energy consump- tion of user . We deﬁne","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"3) Consumption Boundary: We set consumption boundaries for each time slot, considering the conditions such as there are appliances that have to be always on, which causes must-run loads [20], and there exists power limitation. In time slot s, let B_i^s and b_i^s denote the maximum and minimum consumption limits for user i, respectively. We make \\mathbf{B}_i \\triangleq (B_i^1,B_i^2,\\ldots,B_i^S) and \\mathbf{b}_i \\triangleq (b_i^1,b_i^2,\\ldots,b_i^S) denote the maximum and minimum consumption limits for user i in all time slots, respectively. 4) Feasible Consumption: Let x_i^s denote the power consumption of user i in time slot s. We define \\mathbf{x}_i \\triangleq (x_i^1,x_i^2,\\ldots,x_i^S) as the vector of energy consumption of user i. We define X_i \\triangleq \\{\\mathbf{x}_i \\mid b_i^s \\le x_i^s \\le B_i^s, \\forall s \\in \\mathcal{S}\\} to be user i's feasible energy consumption set. As in [3], we also assume that user would like to shift their energy consumption in response to the price values [21]-[23]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e09","location":{"location_id":"src-d83df5b2d1-p03-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model / B. Energy Cost Model","anchor_text":"Assumption 3: There exists a differentiable, convex, nonde- creasing function , in which . For each ","equation_id":"(1), (2)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Assumption 1: The cost function is monotonously increasing to the aggregate offered energy capacity. Assumption 2: The cost function is strictly convex. Assumption 3: There exists a differentiable, convex, nondecreasing function c_s(t), in which t \\in [0,+\\infty). For each s \\in \\mathcal{S}, c_s(0) \\ge 0 and as t \\to \\infty, c_s(t) \\to \\infty. And it can be written such that for t \\ge 0, C_s(t) = \\int_{0}^{t} c_s(z)\\,dz. (1) We use the following quadratic function which satisfies Assumptions 1-3 as the cost function [10], [28] to represent the cost of providing T_s units of energy in time slot s by the energy provider. C_s(T_s) = h_{s1}T_s^2 + h_{s2}T_s + h_{s3}, (2) where h_{s1} \\ge 0, h_{s2} \\ge 0, and h_{s3} \\ge 0 are the fixed parameters."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e11","location":{"location_id":"src-d83df5b2d1-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Mechanism Design and the AGV Mechanism / A. Basic Definitions and Qualifications","anchor_text":"ne p- Consider a public system including agents: . Each agent holds its private information 3], that","equation_id":"(3)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Consider a public system including I agents: \\{1,2,\\ldots,I\\}. Each agent i \\in \\{1,2,\\ldots,I\\} holds its private information \\theta_i \\in \\Theta_i that is not detectable by others. A mechanism \\mathcal{M} represented by tuple (F,TS) consists of a pair of functions F and TS. F: \\Theta_1 \\times \\Theta_2 \\times \\cdots \\times \\Theta_I \\to O is the allocation rule, where O represents a set of possible outcomes. TS: \\Theta_1 \\times \\Theta_2 \\times \\cdots \\times \\Theta_I \\to \\mathbb{R}^I is the transfer payment rule, where \\mathbb{R}^I represents a set of agents' transfer payments. The set of agents' transfer payments is \\{ts_1,\\ldots,ts_I\\}, in which ts_i is the transfer payment of agent i. The utility of agent i: u_i(F(\\hat{\\theta}_i,\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}),\\theta_i) depends on the outcome F(\\hat{\\theta}_i,\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}) and the true information of agent i: \\theta_i. \\hat{\\theta}_i denotes the reported information of agent i, as opposed to \\theta_i. \\hat{\\theta}_{-i} = \\{\\hat{\\theta}_1,\\ldots,\\hat{\\theta}_{i-1},\\hat{\\theta}_{i+1},\\ldots,\\hat{\\theta}_I\\} denotes the truthfully reported information of all other agents. So the function of agent i's total payoff or welfare is written as: p_i(\\hat{\\theta}_i,\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}) = u_i[F(\\hat{\\theta}_i,\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}),\\theta_i] + ts_i(\\hat{\\theta}_i,\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}). (3)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e009","location":{"location_id":"src-d83df5b2d1-p06-c1r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Problem Formulation and Mechanism Solution / C. Proposed Mechanism","anchor_text":"1384 where is an arbitrary function of and is the transfer payment of user . We use the following fu","equation_id":"(21)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"where h_i is an arbitrary function of \\hat{\\theta}_{-i} and ts_i(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}) is the transfer payment of user i. We use the following function (which is introduced in [31]) to be h_i. \\[ h_i(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}) = \\sum_{m\\in\\mathcal{N}_{-i}} U_m\\left(\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}} x_m^s(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i})\\right) - \\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}} C_s\\left(\\sum_{m\\in\\mathcal{N}_{-i}} x_m^s(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i})\\right), \\tag{21} \\] where \\mathbf{x}(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}) is the AGV allocation choice in (19) when user i is excluded from the system."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e010","location":{"location_id":"src-d83df5b2d1-p06-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Problem Formulation and Mechanism Solution / C. Proposed Mechanism","anchor_text":"where is the AGV allocation choice in (19) when user is excluded from the system. 5) Transfer Paymen","equation_id":"(22)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"5) Transfer Payment: We introduce two kinds of transfer payment for ts_i(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}) in (20). The normal transfer payment in AGV mechanism is in the form such that \\[ ts_i^{norm}(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}) = -E_{\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}} \\left\\{ \\sum_{m\\in\\mathcal{N}_{-i}} U_m\\left(\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}} x_m^s(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i})\\right) - \\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}} C_s\\left(\\sum_{m\\in\\mathcal{N}_{-i}} x_m^s(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i})\\right) \\right\\} + \\frac{1}{N-1}\\sum_{j\\neq i}^{N} E_{\\hat{\\theta}_{-j}} \\left\\{ \\sum_{m\\in\\mathcal{N}_{-j}} U_m\\left(\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}} x_m^s(\\hat{\\theta}_{-j})\\right) - \\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}} C_s\\left(\\sum_{m\\in\\mathcal{N}_{-j}} x_m^s(\\hat{\\theta}_{-j})\\right) \\right\\}, \\tag{22} \\] which satisfies (6), i.e., \\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{N}} ts_i^{norm}(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}) = 0. The first term of ts_i^{norm} is the expected social objective among users j\\neq i when user i reports its information \\hat{\\theta}_i, with the assumption that all other agents report true information. With problem (19) reduced from (18), the payment in (20) minus ts_i(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}) has been changed into a marginal cost, and the effect of utility function on the payment is changed to be in the form of transfer payment."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e013","location":{"location_id":"src-d83df5b2d1-p06-c1r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Problem Formulation and Mechanism Solution / C. Proposed Mechanism","anchor_text":"which satiﬁes (6), i.e., . The ﬁrst term of is the expected social objective among users when fouser","equation_id":"(23)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"In order to guarantee that users consume energy as what they declares, we add a part that represents a punishment for the inconsistency of user’s declaration and behavior. The new transfer payment that avoids user’s cheating in power consumption still satisfies (6). Here, \\[ ts_i^{enhanced}(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}) = r_1 \\cdot ts_i^{norm}(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}) + ct_i(\\theta_{-i}, \\hat{\\theta}_{-i}), \\tag{23} \\] where r_1 is a parameter to control the influence of the basic transfer payment, and ct_i(\\theta_{-i}, \\hat{\\theta}_{-i}) is a function that sets the cost for user to cheat. ct_i(\\theta_{-i}, \\hat{\\theta}_{-i}) is in the form of (24)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e014","location":{"location_id":"src-d83df5b2d1-p06-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Problem Formulation and Mechanism Solution / C. Proposed Mechanism","anchor_text":"transfer payment, and is a function that sets the cost for user to cheat. is in the form of (24) whe","equation_id":"(24)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"\\[ ct_i(\\theta_{-i}, \\hat{\\theta}_{-i}) = r_2 \\left\\{ -E_{\\theta_{-i}}\\{\\phi(-i)\\} + \\frac{1}{N-1}\\sum_{i\\neq i}^{N} E_{\\theta_{-i}}\\{\\phi(-i)\\} \\right\\}, \\tag{24} \\] where r_2 is a parameter that controls the influence of the cheat cost. The first part of ct_i(\\theta_{-i}, \\hat{\\theta}_{-i}) is the expected total punishment for users j\\neq i. Here, \\hat{x}_i is user i’s declared total amount of daily power consumption, and x_i is the real one according to the consumptive information known by the provider after that day."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e015","location":{"location_id":"src-d83df5b2d1-p06-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Problem Formulation and Mechanism Solution / C. Proposed Mechanism","anchor_text":"he p y y 24) st. Here, is an expectation representing the incon- for sistency of user’s declaration ","equation_id":"(25)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"\\[ \\phi(-i) = \\begin{cases} \\left[\\sum_{m\\in\\mathcal{N}_{-i}}(x_m-\\hat{x}_m)\\right]^2 & \\text{if } \\left[\\sum_{m\\in\\mathcal{N}_{-i}}(x_m-\\hat{x}_m)\\right]>0, \\\\ 0 & \\text{else}. \\end{cases} \\tag{25} \\]"}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":null,"empty_x_reason":null,"empty_theta_reason":null},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-bidding","v141-a-M01-clearing","v141-a-M01-coordination","v141-a-M01-participation","v141-a-M01-pricing","v141-a-M01-settlement","v141-a-M01-validation"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e005","quote":"We define \\theta_i = \\{\\mathbf{B}_i, \\mathbf{b}_i, \\mathbf{x}_i\\} and \\hat{\\theta}_i = \\{\\hat{\\mathbf{B}}_i, \\hat{\\mathbf{b}}_i, \\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_i\\}, respectively, to denote the true and declared demand parameters"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e008","quote":"The payments are structured such that \\[ t_i(\\hat{\\theta}) = -\\left[ \\sum_{m\\in\\mathcal{N}_{-i}} U_m\\left(\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}} x_m^s(\\hat{\\theta})\\right) - \\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}} C_s\\left(\\sum_{m\\in\\mathcal{N}} x_m^s(\\hat{\\theta})\\right) \\right] + h_i(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}) + ts_i(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}), \\tag{20} \\]"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e011","quote":"1. Registered users submit the energy demand for the next consumption cycle. * Each user selects the types of appliances and decides each type’s energy demand. The smart meter computes \\hat{x}_i and \\hat{\\psi}_i. * \\hat{\\theta}_i = \\{\\mathbf{B}_i, \\mathbf{b}_i, \\mathbf{x}_i\\}, \\psi_i and user’s consumption record will be submitted to the energy provider. 2. The energy provider optimizes users’ consumption allocations and set the payments."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e008","quote":"The payments are structured such that \\[ t_i(\\hat{\\theta}) = -\\left[ \\sum_{m\\in\\mathcal{N}_{-i}} U_m\\left(\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}} x_m^s(\\hat{\\theta})\\right) - \\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}} C_s\\left(\\sum_{m\\in\\mathcal{N}} x_m^s(\\hat{\\theta})\\right) \\right] + h_i(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}) + ts_i(\\hat{\\theta}_{-i}), \\tag{20} \\]"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e006","quote":"For a given vector of the declared demand information \\hat{\\theta} = \\{\\hat{\\theta}_1,\\ldots,\\hat{\\theta}_N\\}, the AGV mechanism chooses the consumption allocation \\mathbf{x}(\\hat{\\theta}) as the optimal solution to (15)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e001","quote":"Hence, we consider a social objective, which is to maximize the social benefit while minimizing the cost of providing that amount of electricity."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e007","quote":"We suppose that the energy provider does not change the declared amount of every user’s daily power consumption. We only optimize users’ hourly consumptive distribution."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e01","quote":"One will be punished to pay extra if there is a cheat record in its consumption history."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e03","quote":"User who reveals false information will have to pay much more in the following operation cycles."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e021","quote":"This means, in the AGV mechanism, those who gain higher utilities by using the same unit of energy have to pay more."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e017","quote":"This implies that the transfer function can realize a payment reallocation among users, and no extra payment is required to be paid by the system."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"abaa621955e18655aef7cb99fcbce1796e985a8d781810e87955da8da0dc6525","applicability":"formalized","reason":"Payment rule (20), the zero-sum transfer condition, the cheat-penalty term (25), and the enhanced transfer equation (23) are transcribed from unreviewed candidate evidence. The explicit cheat-cost equation (24) is not formalized because the supplied transcription has ambiguous summation and expectation indices, so C004 remains not_formalized. No independent source audit or scientific acceptance is claimed.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-VAR-x_i_s","node_ids":["clearing.social_welfare","settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R006-E003"],"rationale":"x_m^s(\\hat\\theta) is the allocation produced by the social-welfare clearing rule and evaluated in the payment rule; E003 links clearing.social_welfare to settlement.payment."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-VAR-t_i","node_ids":["bidding.demand","clearing.social_welfare","settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R006-E001","v141-c-R006-E003"],"rationale":"t_i is the payment output of rule (20); it depends on declared demand through E001 and on the allocation from social-welfare clearing through E003."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-VAR-ts_i","node_ids":["bidding.demand","settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R006-E001"],"rationale":"ts_i(\\hat\\theta_{-i}) is a transfer-payment component driven by other users' declared demand information, represented by E001."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-VAR-ts_i_enhanced","node_ids":["bidding.demand","settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R006-E001"],"rationale":"The enhanced transfer payment is part of settlement and depends on declared information; E001 links declared demand to payment."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-VAR-ts_i_norm","node_ids":["settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The normal AGV transfer payment is a base settlement component used in the enhanced transfer payment."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-VAR-x_i_total","node_ids":["settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Real total daily consumption is recorded and used in the history-based penalty within settlement."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-PAR-theta_hat","node_ids":["bidding.demand","settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R006-E001"],"rationale":"The declared demand-parameter profile is submitted in bidding and is an input to payment; E001 links bidding.demand to settlement.payment."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-PAR-theta_minus_i","node_ids":["settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Others' information profile enters budget-balance and transfer-payment expressions in settlement."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-PAR-U_m","node_ids":["clearing.social_welfare","settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R006-E003"],"rationale":"The utility function is part of the social-welfare objective and is evaluated at the clearing allocation in payment rule (20); E003 links clearing to settlement."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-PAR-C_s","node_ids":["clearing.social_welfare","settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R006-E003"],"rationale":"The cost function is part of the social-welfare objective and is evaluated at the clearing allocation in payment rule (20); E003 links clearing to settlement."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-PAR-h_i","node_ids":["bidding.demand","settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R006-E001"],"rationale":"h_i is an arbitrary function of other users' declared parameters in payment rule (20); E001 links declared demand to payment."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-PAR-N","node_ids":["settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"N is the number of users used in the transfer-payment summation."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-PAR-x_hat_i","node_ids":["bidding.demand","settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R006-E001"],"rationale":"Declared total daily consumption is submitted demand information used in the cheat penalty; E001 links bidding to payment."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-PAR-r_1","node_ids":["settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"r_1 controls the weight on the normal transfer payment in the enhanced transfer payment."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-PAR-ct_i","node_ids":["bidding.demand","settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R006-E001"],"rationale":"The cheat-cost function depends on declared and true information and enters the enhanced transfer payment; E001 links declared demand to payment."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-C006","node_ids":["settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The budget-balance sum of transfer payments is a property of the settlement.payment node; no dedicated edge exists in the frozen graph."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-C007","node_ids":["bidding.demand","clearing.social_welfare","settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R006-E001","v141-c-R006-E003"],"rationale":"Payment rule (20) consumes declared demand through E001 and the social-welfare allocation through E003, producing settlement.payment."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-C008","node_ids":["bidding.demand","settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R006-E001"],"rationale":"The cheat penalty compares declared and real total consumption; declared demand enters settlement through E001."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-C009","node_ids":["bidding.demand","settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R006-E001"],"rationale":"The enhanced transfer payment uses declared information and is a settlement payment component; E001 links bidding to settlement."}],"revision_requests":["Obtain verified B extraction of equation (24) to resolve the printed summation index `\\sum_{i\\neq i}^{N}` and the repeated expectation term; then formalize ct_i and complete the history-penalty constraint C004.","Verify whether `t_i` in (20) and `ts_i` in (17)/(20)/(23) are real-valued and clarify notation among `ts_i`, `ts_i^{norm}`, and `ts_i^{enhanced}`.","Confirm the Method 1 versus Method 2 scope of \\hat\\theta: Method 1 includes \\psi_i, while Method 2 defines \\theta_i=\\{\\mathbf B_i,\\mathbf b_i,\\mathbf x_i\\}; C001 is scoped to Method 2 but payment rule (20) is stated generally.","Provide verified definitions of h_i from equation (21) and the normal transfer payment from equation (22) for a complete payment-rule formalization; this output represents them only as declared functions/objects with source references."]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
